From: FliperOS <fliperos@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] ggpo: the network every millisecond, apart from the frames (FliperOS)

Flycast gave GGPO the network only in ggpo_idle, once a frame, and added
the local input after it, so the input left a frame later: on a cable with
less than 1 ms of round trip GGPO measured 20 to 110 ms of ping. That ping
sets how far ahead each side runs, so the faster machine ran 3 to 7 frames
ahead, predicted that many frames of the other's input (near GGPO's limit
of 8, where it stalls) and waited a frame every few seconds.

ggpo_poll_network() does only the network half of ggpo_idle (read what
arrived, send what waits; no rollback), and a GGPO-net thread calls it
every millisecond with try_lock on the GGPO mutex, so it never holds up a
frame or stopSession. A disconnection seen there ends the session at the
next frame. On Windows the timer goes to 1 ms while the session runs.
Tested with two Flycast on one PC: ping 1 to 3 ms instead of 4 to 51.
---
diff --git a/core/deps/ggpo/include/ggponet.h b/core/deps/ggpo/include/ggponet.h
index 9bf8552..0fa136e 100644
--- a/core/deps/ggpo/include/ggponet.h
+++ b/core/deps/ggpo/include/ggponet.h
@@ -477,6 +477,18 @@ GGPO_API GGPOErrorCode __cdecl ggpo_set_frame_delay(GGPOSession *,
 GGPO_API GGPOErrorCode __cdecl ggpo_idle(GGPOSession *,
                                          int timeout);
 
+/*
+ * ggpo_poll_network --
+ * FliperOS: reads the packets that arrived and sends the ones waiting, and
+ * nothing else: no rollback, no game callback but the network events
+ * (connected, synchronized, interrupted, disconnected). Meant for a thread
+ * that calls it every millisecond, so that inputs and acknowledgements do
+ * not wait for the next ggpo_idle (one frame on each side) and the ping is
+ * the network's. Never during a rollback; the caller serializes it with the
+ * other GGPO calls.
+ */
+GGPO_API GGPOErrorCode __cdecl ggpo_poll_network(GGPOSession *);
+
 /*
  * ggpo_add_local_input --
  *
